# Position adjustments: layer_positions

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

ggplot2's reference page for
[`layer_positions`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/layer_positions.html) has no
examples, so this notebook says only what the page describes and where Giggly stands.

A layer's position adjusts where its marks sit, mostly to keep them from overlapping: dodging sets
them side by side, stacking piles them up, filling scales each pile to a proportion, jittering adds
random noise and nudging offsets them by a fixed distance. ggplot2 takes a layer's position from a
geom's default, as a string (`position = "jitter"`), as a `position_*()` function, which also
carries its options, or through `layer()`; for many layers it is `position_identity()`, which leaves
them where they are. Giggly takes a layer's position by name, `position: :jitter`, or by name with
its options in a tuple, `position: {:jitter, seed: 1}`, where ggplot2 calls a `position_*()`
function. Every layer function takes the positions that can move its marks, as ggplot2's do:
`:identity`, stacking (`:stack` or `{:stack, options}`), filling (`:fill` or `{:fill, options}`,
as `position_fill()` does), dodging (`:dodge` or `{:dodge, options}`, and `:dodge2` or
`{:dodge2, options}`, as `position_dodge2()` does), jittering (`:jitter` or `{:jitter, options}`)
and nudging (`:nudge`). Marks with no width of their own, such as points, text and line ranges,
are dodged, either way, only within a width given; box plots and rectangles are neither stacked, filled nor
jittered, and horizontal and vertical lines are only nudged. Bars and histograms stack by default, as do
`Giggly.Plot.geom_area/3`, and box plots dodge as `position_dodge2()` does;
`Giggly.Plot.geom_jitter/3` jitters its points, and `Giggly.Plot.geom_text/3` nudges every label,
as ggplot2's `geom_text()` does. A nudged layer moves by its `nudge_x:` and `nudge_y:` options or a
mapped `nudge_x` and `nudge_y`, as ggplot2 4.0's do, so `:nudge` takes no options, where
`position_nudge()` also takes `x` and `y`. Not in Giggly yet: `position_jitterdodge()` and
`layer()`.